(Battambang): Ms. Chim Chanthen, 41 years old, is a member of a small study farmer group and a member of a vegetable business group living in Popal Khe village, Otaki commune, Thmor Kol district, Battambang province.
She lives with four family members and her occupation and income are from farming and growing vegetables. Prior to joining ASPIRE, we conducted a small-scale study group of farmers promoting a new agricultural technology program that resists climate change (ASPIRE) in 2016.
In addition to selling fried bananas and working as a construction worker, her family also farms regularly, but the benefits of farming are insufficient compared to the cost of fertilizers and labor.
During 2015, on an area of one and a half acres or about 2,400 square meters, Ms. Chanthen said that the income from farming on the land is only about 5 bags. Only.
Due to insufficient income and regular expenses for her entire family, she also discussed with her husband to migrate to find work in Thailand.
"The income is very low and can not support the family, and we do not have any skills, do not know what to do, so we decided to migrate," she said.
Fortunately, in 2016, ASPIRE program recruited farmers and set up a small group of farmers to study in Otaki commune, Thmor Kol district, Battambang province. Also selected as a presenter for rice production.
"Agriculture came down in 2016 and I joined them to learn, because I also love and like farming," she said.
During the training, Ms. Chanthen seemed to be very interested in growing vegetables, and she also met with technical officials to get more guidance, it will be clear in her mind that the shift from large-scale rice farming to focus. Growing vegetables in large numbers will not fail.
Mr. In Sovanmony, Deputy Director of the Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries of Battambang Province, said that in general, the department urges farmers to take up any business, the department looks at their geographical location, if the area is covered with livestock, it will work. Encourage farmers to raise animals mainly, but the rice side will also encourage more to turn to rice.
Mr. Sovann Mony added, "Wherever we grow rice, we provide rice. Wherever there is potential for vegetable crops, we push the potential of vegetable crops to them, and how do we connect the technology and the market to the district?" His's.
After receiving advice, she and her family decided to convert from farmland to farmland, spending $ 1,500 on land reclamation. In 2017, the Thmor Koul district agriculture department of ASPIRE decided to select his family to demonstrate for the production of sweet cucumber vegetables, planted on an area of 2,400 square meters.
What surprised and made Chanthen's family the happiest at that time was the high yield from growing cucumbers. Covering more than 2,000 square meters, she grows 12 tons of cucumbers in just three months and sells for up to $ 3,500.
In 2018, ASPIRE transformed a small group of small farmers into a vegetable business group and built a 350-square-meter, 7-meter-wide and 50-meter-long net house for Chim Chanthen's family.
And through this net house, her family has also expanded the cultivation of safe vegetables in the net house, such as kale, beans, beans, cucumbers, spinach, kale, Thai cabbage and kale.
The life of a fried banana seller, construction worker and migrant life is over, and instead she grows a vegetable business that provides a lot of income and completely facilitates Chan Then's family.
