He said that in Stung Treng province, there was no outbreak of African swine fever, so people should not worry and eat again. The sale of pork in Stung Treng market is professionally checked and does not allow any trader to sell sick pork.
Please note that African swine fever is not a risk to human health, but it is a risk to domestic and wild pigs because the virus can live longer in processed pork, dried pork and pork.
There is currently no cure for the disease and there is no vaccine or effective treatment yet, so when pigs become infected, the only measure is to destroy the affected herd.
However, the outbreak of African swine fever has caused pork sellers in Stung Treng province's Samaki market to complain that it is not selling well because consumers are afraid of the disease.
At the same time, Ms. Seila, a pork seller in Samaki Market, Stung Treng Province, described that for the past 10 days, she has not been so isolated, it is less than before. Previously sold from 100 kg to 150 kg, now sold only more than 40 kg.
A report from the Ministry of Health says that since the outbreak of the African swine fever, a total of 2,960 pigs have died in Cambodia so far.
