This measure will be implemented nationwide from July 19, 2019, with the requirement to find a helmet and then allow to travel without penalty for one month from July 19, 2019. Brick kiln owners and sellers in Svay Rieng worry about business collapse due to smuggled bricks from Vietnam through corridors
(Svay Rieng): The owner of a brick kiln, as well as Khmer brick sellers in Svay Rieng province, complained and worried that their business would collapse because unscrupulous traders smuggled bricks from Vietnam to sell in the province without payment. Taxes make it difficult for Cambodian bricks to compete.
Craftsmen and traders complained to Fresh News that today dozens of brick trucks using Vietnamese and Cambodian license plates were delivering bricks to Bavet and some districts in Svay Rieng province. Craftsmen and traders claim that the Vietnamese bricks were not taxed by the state, in accordance with the border authorities' permission to import them.
Khmer brick makers point out that their brick kilns have the proper permits from the relevant ministries and authorities, as well as pay regular taxes to the state. Vietnamese bricks, on the other hand, do not pay taxes to the state, with traders running to pay only the head of the truck for the authorities to stand in the corridor.
Vendors claim that the price of a piece of brick imported by Vietnamese traders is about 20 percent cheaper than Cambodian bricks because they do not pay taxes to the state, making Vietnamese bricks cheaper. On the other hand, the cost of Cambodian labor, water and electricity are still more expensive than Vietnam, so the more expensive Cambodian bricks can not compete in the market with Vietnam.
Responding to the suggestion that the import of bricks from Vietnam to meet the demand for bricks due to the growth of construction in Svay Rieng, the traders claimed that the brick kilns in Svay Rieng and Neak Leung, Prey Veng today can supply construction in both provinces, despite the increase Of the construction of many high-rise buildings in Bavet, however.
The brick kiln traders appealed to the relevant authorities, especially Svay Rieng provincial governor Men Vibol, to take action to prevent the import of tax evaded bricks from Vietnam. They said that if allowed to import, the right to pay taxes to the state so that traders can compete to sell bricks in the market equally and affordablely, especially to the state to increase the national economy.
According to the traders, in the case of the Vietnamese bricks that are invading Svay Rieng province to seize the Cambodian brick market, they have asked for intervention from the Svay Rieng provincial authorities many times, but to no avail, still see the presence of dirty trucks. Continue to import and sell in the same province.
