Legal Awareness: Intellectual Property on "Trademarks"
(Phnom Penh): Trade is an indispensable sector in the development of the country, especially for countries that adhere to the free market economy. After Cambodia changed its planned economy into a free market economy, we have seen a significant increase in trade activity in the Cambodian market.
At the same time, many problems have arisen in this area, such as trademark infringement or dishonest market competition.
Therefore, in order to protect your brand or trademark (name of company or enterprise) or other services, you need to be aware of the advantages of registering and the disadvantages of not registering a trademark or brand as stated in the trademark law and acts of Dishonest competition.
Last week, the Legal Awareness Program, a collaboration between Fresh News and the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation, raised the topic of "brands" and distributed them to citizens as well as students this week. We would like to bring the topic related to intellectual property on "trademark" to present to the people:
What is a trademark? Which trademarks are registered and protected by law?
At the same time, some "trademarks" are prohibited under national law from registering for protection. In the spirit of Article 20 of the Trademark Law and acts of dishonest competition, trademarks are prohibited from being registered as follows:
Names or names or marks are not to be used commercially if the substance or use of the designated name or mark is detrimental to public order or morals.
In particular, the definition of a name or a noun does not meet the distinctive features of each type of enterprise, which confuses the business community or the public.
Example: The name "Pailin Jewelry" is not an appropriate name for a company if it does not sell gem jewelry from the Pailin area. That is, if we name it like that, it may confuse the public by thinking that the jewelry is brought from Pailin province.
In addition to the prohibition of some trademarks (names) that can not be registered, separately according to Article 21 of the same law also provides protection for individuals who have been violated as follows:
• Although laws or regulations stipulate the obligation to register trademarks, those names are protected against illegal acts by third parties, whether pre-registered or unregistered.
Subsequent use of trademarks by third parties, whether trademarks or trademarks or collective trademarks, or any use which may mislead the public is considered illegal.
In short, in order to protect the trademark of a company or enterprise that is its work, the owner of the company or enterprise must take the trademark he created to register the trademark with the Ministry of Commerce to avoid abuse by partners or crooks in the circumstances. Any eventually.
