Minister of Agriculture visits the community forest "Khmum Srok Thlok Russey" in Kampong Svay district, Kampong Thom province, there are 83 forest communities throughout the province!
(Kampong Thom): Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, together with Mr. Nhek Ban Kheng, Deputy Governor of Kampong Thom Province, this morning, July 21, 2019, visited the “Khmum Srok Thlok Russey” community forest. It is located in Trapeang Thmor village, Trapeang Russey commune, Kampong Svay district, Kampong Thom province.
According to the Forestry Administration, there are 83 community forests in Kampong Thom province with an area of more than 83,000 hectares, of which 69 communities with an area of more than 59,000 hectares are registered by the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries. .
Separately, the bee forest community of Srok Tlok Russey was established on November 19, 2008. Most of the communities are mainly engaged in agriculture and exploration of forest products and by-products on an area of more than 671 hectares, covered with a variety of trees, providing shade, a paradise for the livelihood of the community, which depends in part on natural resources. Worth this.
Taking this opportunity, the Minister had a talk with the community, bringing the greetings of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister, Samdech Kittipritthbandit, Samdech President of the National Assembly, the President of the Senate and some other top leaders to all communities. Samdech wished every family happiness and prosperity.
Mr. Veng Sakhon also praised the leaders of the Forestry Administration, Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, local authorities who have worked together to create this community forest to maintain and develop the forest as a natural resource.
"We have established it, so we must work together to preserve the forest and the health of the family economy, the community, both now and in the future," he said. Therefore, what can strengthen and expand their livelihoods if we work together to develop this community to grow by creating a livelihood, expanding agriculture, raising animals (chickens and cows) and growing crops that yield in Short and long time, such as bamboo.
The Minister instructed the Forestry Administration and the Department to provide additional training on new agricultural techniques, including forest management, administration, credit and community development, to turn the area into an ecotourism site in the near future. Exploiting the potential of this natural forest to increase income, reduce poverty and migration.
He said the forestry administration should promote the registration of more community forests to benefit the management and protection of the remaining forests for future generations. Proper ownership of community forest land must be made to avoid illegal logging and encroachment on community forest land.
