73 BELTEI students and students bring good knowledge of international camp in South Korea to their homeland
The international camp is attended by more than 4,000 young people from 80 countries around the world, as well as education ministers from 22 countries.
On his return to Cambodia on the night of July 20, 2019, Mr. Ly Bun Chhay, Deputy Director General of BELTEI Group and Chairman of the Branch Management Team, thanked the International Youth Federation (IYF) for its cooperation and providing opportunities for students. BELTEI has participated in an international camp in the Republic of Korea, and this is the sixth time that BELTEI International School has sent a total of 546 students to participate in this program.
Mr. Ly Bun Chhay said that the participation in this program is focused on leadership, youth orientation in the 21st century and learning about the context of modern science education, and all 73 BELTEI students visited to explore the area. Many important places such as Seoul, Busan, historical sites and other tourist sites as well.
He added that the purpose and benefit of participating in this camp is that BELTEI wants its students to have the courage to adapt to live and work in groups with other nations to learn from each other's experiences. Like changing new cultures, traditions, customs and other new knowledge that they have never encountered.
He said that the participation in this camp also shows the ability of Cambodian students on the international stage who have the ability to participate in international programs, as well as show the world more about the traditions, customs, culture, civilization and development of the country. Cambodia as well.
On that occasion, Lim Seavmey, an 11th grade student, described that after attending the 14-day international camp in Korea, he was very happy to participate in many events.
In this program, Xiaomei learned about communicating with many ethnic groups using English, working in groups and playing popular games, etc. From many important areas such as Seoul, Busan and other historical sites as well.
Meanwhile, Thong Sung Hui, an 11th grade student at the 6th branch, said that the camp gave him the courage to take responsibility for himself, to adapt to living and working in a team. Stay with different nationalities and learn about the new contexts of developed countries, which are new knowledge in self-development as well as national development for the future.
