42 restaurant and canteen owners receive training on good hygiene and food safety practices
(Phnom Penh): The Department of Medicine, Food, Medical Equipment and Cosmetics of the Ministry of Health, in collaboration with the Mekong Institute, funded by New Zealand, organized a training course on food hygiene and safety practices for 42 restaurant and restaurant owners and managers. Coming from 39 restaurants and eateries in Phnom Penh.
The training was held this week under the chairmanship of Dr. Heng Bun Kounkiet, Director of the Department of Pharmacology, Food, Medical Equipment and Cosmetics of the Ministry of Health and with the participation of Pharmacist Ang Hok Srun, Director of the Food Safety Office of the Ministry of Health at the Phnom Penh Hotel. .
Speaking at the training, Mr. Ang Hok Srun presented to the training course on the outbreak of food poisoning in the country from 2015 to 2019, which caused the outbreak of food poisoning. To lose a lot of family and national budget, even affect your business.
He added that the purpose of today's training is to present to you who have been in the restaurant and canteen business to understand in depth some important points to participate in preventing and reducing food poisoning as well. Expanding and increasing the number of tourists in its business as well.
Speaking on that occasion, Dr. Heng Bunkiet recommended the training course on some important points such as:
First, the selection of raw materials such as vegetables, fish, meat, seafood, spices for food preparation must be hygienic, quality and safe and have a clear source of production and sales
Second: the use of clean water for washing vegetables, fish, meat, dishes, pots, spoons, etc. In addition, water is also an important raw material in cooking and other production.
Third, keep raw materials and food products in a clean warehouse or room with labels and in a safe temperature.
Fifth, clean the cooking area, guest place to eat, hand wash area, clean the toilet and have enough hand soap, clean paper towels and avoid pets in the food store.
Sixth: All staff involved in food preparation, cooking, serving customers must understand hygiene and have good work rules.
Seventh, keep food in the refrigerator at a safe temperature and cook well.
It should be noted that at the end of this training, 42 participants received certificates to participate in the course. On that occasion, the owners and representatives of 10 other restaurants came to receive a certificate of good hygiene practices from the Ministry of Health.
