Residents find a total of 10 landmines and mortar shells buried in the area of the former battlefield of Svay Chek district, Banteay Meanchey province
(Banteay Meanchey): Residents found a total of 10 anti-personnel mines and mortar shells buried in plantations at two different locations in the former war zone in Svay Chek district, Banteay Meanchey province, and were collected by local police to keep Wait for it to be handed over to the CMAC officer to be destroyed to avoid endangering the lives of the people in the event of any bad event.
Landmines and unexploded ordnance found in Svay Chek district, Banteay Meanchey province on the morning of July 18, 2019 at a cassava plantation in Ponsay Cheung village, Svay Chek commune and another plantation in Ou Veng Cheung village, Ta Ben commune. Svay Chek District, Banteay Meanchey Province.
Colonel Chea Sloh, police inspector of Svay Chek district, said that the 10 landmines and unexploded ordnance were 9 anti-personnel mines and a 130 mm mortar shell. The seeds were found by two villagers on a cassava plantation and in a rice field while the cassava grass was being plowed and plowed.
He added that the bullets had been stored in a safe place to be handed over to CMAC officers for destruction.
Please be reminded that in Banteay Meanchey province, on July 8, 414 unexploded ordnance (UXO) and anti-aircraft and anti-personnel bombs were handed over to demolition experts HelloTrak for safekeeping. Safe place. After the villagers in Phnom Srok district found the bullets buried in a plantation, which is a former army barracks in the former battlefield in the village of Trapeang Thmor Khang Cheung, Poi Char commune, Phnom Srok district.
It should be noted that Cambodia, despite the end of the war, mines and remnants of war are still buried in and on the ground in areas that have not yet been cleared, which poses a risk to the lives of the people. Around this issue, Cambodia still has an area threatened by landmines and unexploded ordnance of about 2,148 square kilometers.
The Royal Government has set 2025 as the year to end the threat posed by landmines and unexploded ordnance.
According to the report from the competent authorities, in the first six months of 2019, there were 61 victims of landmines and unexploded ordnance left over from the war, an increase of 30 compared to In the same period in 2018, there were only 31 victims. Businesses created by 5 Cambodian children will be featured in the Small and Medium Business Program in Cambodia 2019
