(Phnom Penh): Citizens and Buddhists at the foot of Kampong Kdol pagoda in 7 villages expressed their dissatisfaction with the appointment of the new chief monk, Bhikkhu Rom Saran, at Kampong Kdol pagoda, located in Sangkat Sak Sampov, Khan Dangkor, Phnom Penh. The people demand the election of the new pagoda chief from the people living in the district.
The villagers stated that they did not know about the appointment of the new chief abbot of Kampong Kdol pagoda and that the authorities did not inform the pagoda committee, which was quietly appointed, so the people were not satisfied. The country has the law, the family has the rules, and before appointing the pagoda's chief priest, the Buddhist relatives must also know or vote for the satisfaction of the people.
(Phnom Penh): The Documentation Center of Cambodia will launch a new book on the experiences of survivors of the World War II massacre, which will take place on July 19, 2019 at the Contemporary Art Gallery of the Leaf Institute in The event will be attended by Ms. Tun Saim, Undersecretary of State, Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports, as well as representatives of the US and Swedish Embassies in Cambodia.
The launch of this book is a reminder of the Khmer Rouge regime that collapsed 40 years ago and coincides with the end of the European Holocaust 70 years ago, and also coincides with the Convention on the Prevention and Control of the Khmer Rouge. Sanctions for the crime of genocide, adopted and ratified by the United Nations General Assembly on December 9, 1948.
The same book was also translated from English into Khmer by Ms. So Vichetmetta, a researcher at the Documentation Center of Cambodia and a recent graduate student in International Relations.
Metta explained: "This book is the first to explain the views of the survivors of the massacre in Nazi Germany, which is similar to the story of the survivors of the Khmer regime. Red too. "Millions of victims have been abused, starved, tortured and killed outside the courts."
She added that genocide is a crime against humanity. Therefore, educating the next generation about genocide and massacre is aimed at preventing future racism, ethnic strife, and genocide.
Remembering is the beginning and the end of the mission to seek justice, peace and truth. "The obligation to remember is to begin and end with an individual making decisions about the significance of history, not only to oneself but also to future generations."
In addition to distributing more than 3,000 books, DC-Cam also creates public content in the form of e-books, audio and social media. This translation is funded by the Swedish International Development Cooperation Agency and by the United States Agency for International Development in Cambodia.
