The bill blindly denies the existence of a multi-party democracy in Cambodia. The fact of the matter is that Cambodia has always adhered to multi-party democracy as enshrined in the Cambodian Constitution. Political competition has expanded and elections have been held regularly since 1993.
Cambodia recently held a successful Commune / Sangkat Council Election on June 4, 2017, with 12 parties participating and 7.8 million registered voters in the system. Electronic. On July 29, 2018, Cambodia also successfully held the 6th National Assembly election in a free, fair and successful manner.
Twenty political parties participated in the election. In comparison, turnout rose to a new high of 83.2%, compared to 55% for the 2016 US presidential election. It is a true reflection of the will of the people.
Unfortunately, in the six elections since 1993, none of the elections were free and fair. This is a denial of the unacceptable fact that even the 1993 elections organized by the United Nations, called UNTAC, were not considered free and fair. This is an example of an American hypocrisy against Cambodia.
Cambodia must accept the uncomfortable fact that Cambodia cannot and will not persuade any country that has a preconceived notion and good intentions for this small country.
A simple question here is who is most appropriate to judge free and fair elections in Cambodia?
