It should be recalled that in the 1960s, when the country gained independence from France, the Royal Government of Sangkum Reastr Niyum worked hard to promote development throughout the country, especially in Phnom Penh. Stealing power to oppress people at will. Sangvan is a young man living in this village, most of the villagers live in selfishness and the village-commune authorities often exploit, confiscate the rice of the villagers and rape the villagers' wives and children.
All those injustices made Sangvan, a young teacher, hate the oppression and rise up against it. Out of resentment and illegal use of power, the commune authorities arrested Sangvan and shot him dead on July 17, 1965. Coincidentally, Sangwan was shot dead and tried to escape into hiding in the forest. After this incident, Sangvan changed his name to Tea Banh.
The government of the Khmer Republic understands that there are many resistance groups against its regime and plans to dismantle them, including the Tea Banh youth group, which is also mistaken for part of the insurgency. The Republican Army raised troops to destroy Tea Banh's camp, resulting in many deaths and injuries, but was eventually rescued by Ta Say Phu Thong and Ta Chhem (father of Toeun).
