(Tbong Khmum): Mr. Kith Sovannarith, Deputy Director General of the National Bank of Cambodia, called on people to refrain from borrowing money from unlicensed borrowers.
Mr. Kith Sovannarith's appeal was made on the occasion of his participation in the dissemination workshop on microfinance in Cambodia, held on July 16, 2019 in Memot District, Tbong Khmum Province.
Mr. Kith Sovannarith said that people should avoid borrowing money from one place to another, because doing so can make them more and more indebted and will eventually fall into the "debt" situation. "Excessive" because it is beyond the ability to repay.
He added that in order to prevent this problem, the National Bank of Cambodia has cooperated with the relevant departments to prevent the problem of excessive debt through various mechanisms, including through the dissemination workshop on the microfinance sector in Cambodia to educate the financial sector. People who use banking and microfinance services to protect themselves from falling into excessive debt.
He added that at the same time, the National Bank has cooperated with the Ministry of Economy and Finance, the Ministry of Justice and the National Police to take action against companies that provide unauthorized loans.
He urged local authorities to continue to work together to implement the law and take strong action against associations and non-governmental organizations, crooks and unauthorized moneylenders.
Mr. Kith Sovannrith also requested all banks and microfinance institutions to continue to provide financial services with professional ethics, responsibility and transparency in accordance with the Prakas of the National Bank of Cambodia and the Code of Conduct. As well as strengthening the implementation of the principles of consumer protection of the SMART Campaign.
Please note that as of the end of January 2019, there are 74 microfinance institutions, 7 deposit-taking microfinance institutions, and 257 rural credit institutions with a network of operations throughout the country. The loan size is about 23.03 trillion riels (equivalent to 5.75 billion US dollars) is providing about 2.16 million borrowers and the deposit is about 11.70 trillion riels (equivalent to 2. $ 92 billion).
