Canadia Releases $ 20 Million in Financing for the "Women Entrepreneurship Smart Loan" Project of the Cambodian Women Entrepreneurs Association
(Phnom Penh): Canadia Bank has decided to provide a loan of $ 20 million to the "Smart Women Entrepreneur Financing Loan" project of the Cambodian Women Entrepreneurs Association.
On the afternoon of July 16, 2019, Canadia Bank and the Cambodian Women's Association signed a Memorandum of Understanding on this financing project.
Ms. Ing Kantha Phavi, Minister of Women's Affairs, congratulated the Cambodian Women Entrepreneurs Association for its efforts and contribution to the nation's sustainable economic growth through the development of women's entrepreneurship and thanked Canadia Bank for its attention. Focus on contributing to the empowerment of women, especially focusing on cooperation in social responsibility.
She added that empowering women is a win-win strategy that the Royal Government of Cambodia allows the Ministry of Women's Affairs to implement, called economic empowerment, to improve the lives of women and their families.
Mr. Pung Kheav Se, Chairman of the Board of Directors of Canadia Bank, said that Canadia Bank is very pleased to provide support to the Cambodian Women Entrepreneurs Association every year from 2013 to the present.
He added that the two institutions have now reached an agreement to sign an MoU on a $ 20 million financing package.
He said that looking at the significant participation of small and medium enterprises (SMEs) in the national economic development process, Canadia Bank has considered the SME sector as a key strategy and has paid high attention to the development of its products and services. It meets the financial needs of small and medium enterprises, especially business capital and SME business expansion.
Ms. Eng Likong, President of the Cambodian Women Entrepreneurs Association, said that on behalf of the Women Entrepreneurs, we are very pleased with Canadia Bank's support in financing the "$ 20 Million Women Smart Loan" project. .
She said that in the past, finding low-interest loans was difficult for women, which made it difficult for women to maintain and expand their business.
