UN Human Rights Council members voted 18 to 14 last Thursday in favor of a resolution proposing Iceland, with 15 countries abstaining. Through this resolution, UN Human Rights Chair Michelle Bachelet has ordered the preparation of a report on the human rights situation in the Philippines by June 2020.
After the resolution was adopted, spokesman Duterte announced that the Philippines was considering severing diplomatic ties with Iceland and it was unclear whether Philippine authorities would co-operate with UN experts. But what is best known is that until now, Duterte was still very popular among most Filipinos.
Philippine President Rodrigo Duterte, 74, came to power in 2016 and in the same year he declared war on drugs. The war cut off more than 5,300 suspects, while major international human rights groups estimate that nearly 12,000 suspects and more than 164,000 have been arrested.
The extrajudicial killings have been widely criticized internationally, with some human rights groups even suing Duterte at the International Criminal Court. But most Filipinos still support and appreciate the Duterte government.
In fact, the midterm elections in the Philippines in May made it clear that the majority of Filipinos still have the confidence and support of Rodrigo Duterte, a candidate for senator and mayor. The nominee won the election over the opposition.
