137 pigs allowed to be sold in Mean commune after African swine fever was not detected
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Tan Phannara, the Royal Government delegate in charge of the Director General of the General Department of Animal Health and Animal Production, told Fresh News on Sunday, July 14, 2019 that the pigs of the people in Mean commune, Ou Raing Ov district, province. About 137 bees have been allowed to be released after being diagnosed with African swine fever.
Mr. Tan Phannara said that after receiving the measures taken by Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries yesterday, he sent a laboratory machine as well as professional officers to Mean commune where the outbreak occurred. African swine fever and acted to check the pigs of people who want to sell immediately.
Mr. Tan Phannara said that after checking, they found that 137 pigs from 6 families were not infected with African swine fever and were allowed to be released for sale.
Mr. Tan Phannara said that today only the people in Mean commune, Ou Raing Ov district, Tbong Khmum province demanded the examination and diagnosis of pigs for sale. In the other four provinces, however, people are not required to be diagnosed for sale.
However, the professional officials of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries have already informed the people to contact the professional officials at any time to inspect the pigs if any farmers want to release the pigs for sale.
It should be noted that in response to the insistence of pig farmers in the African swine outbreak area who want to sell pigs, on July 13, 2019, Mr. Veng Sakhon issued an urgent measure by sending professional officers and equipment. To diagnose the African swine fever epidemic to examine and diagnose the pigs of people who want to sell.
Measures to solve the current epidemic of swine fever in Cambodia made the people in Mean commune, Ou Raing Ov district, Tbong Khmum province, who sold pigs today very happy and also thanked the emergency measures of Minister of Agriculture as well.
The pig farmers see the minister's move as a smart solution to the problems that lie ahead.
(Kampong Chhnang): Depart from Kampong Chhnang provincial town to Phsar Krom crossing and continue by ferry across the Tonle Sap for about 30-40 minutes, you will reach the other side of Kampong Leng Ti district with pre-Angkorian temples. Some are located.
The trip to the temple area built before the Angkorian era is not only a tour, but you can also learn about the life and daily activities of the people in this agricultural district.
In that area, many big people's houses have appeared, some are under construction, and the road is being expanded, and the village is not as quiet as other areas. Seems to enjoy its daily life under the shadow of peace and development.
Busy describing the general social situation of the people in Kampong Leng district, the team also reached Chrolong village, Dar commune and turn left from the main road for more than 1 km, we will reach a small archeological site with two temples Standing side by side near the slopes of Touk Meas mountain. According to the locals, these two temples are called "Prasat Thnom Pdey" or Prasat Sluti.
According to locals, this tragic site previously had three single towers, but due to the war and genocide of the past, the Khmer Rouge destroyed one of the towers and still two towers collapsed. As a result, it creates sadness for those who visit there.
At present, this archeological site has not been set up as a reception area yet, only the trail that people travel to and from, and tourists can also visit, but there are cucumber, watermelon, soybean and vegetable plantations. There are many other stalls that guests can buy from the farmers or talk about the history of the temple, there are also farmers' huts nearby.
Return to the main road and continue to the north, we will reach the village of Prasat commune and turn right through the gate of Wat Prasat about 1 km, you will see Wat Prasat and drive into The pagoda itself will see a huge tower to the west of the temple, called Prasat Srey, which was built since the 7th century.
This large temple was not much damaged due to the help of monks, abbots as well as Buddhists to preserve and worship, that is, it is still beautifully decorated.
According to the pagoda committee, in the past, there were three temples in the pagoda, but because the Khmer Rouge destroyed all of them, the temple was still clean and the other part was destroyed. In the past, only a large tree was left on the foundation of the temple.
After exploring the temples and cultural treasures in Wat Prasat, you can continue your visit to Prasat Sakl or Prasat Pros, which is located in Chronok Keut village, Chronok commune. This temple is also isolated like other temples, but still in good condition, no major damage, this is a feature of most temples in Kampong Leng district.
Located on an ancient hill in the middle of a rice field north of the mountain, Prasat Sar Khlai seems to be lonely because there are no pagodas, no houses nearby, like Prasat Srey in Wat Prasat.
According to the director of the Kampong Chhnang Provincial Department of Tourism, Mr. Seng Saly, in addition to these three temples, there are other temples in Kampong Leng district.
Mr. Seng Saly confirmed that although there are more tourists as well as people passing through Kampong Leng district, but now there are not many tourists to visit those temples.
