The Council of Ministers decided to build 4 more solar power plants (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): The Council of Ministers in a meeting chaired by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia this morning, July 12, 2019. This has led to the construction of four additional solar power plants with a total capacity of 140 megawatts to generate energy to meet growing demand.
Mr. Phay Siphan, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and Spokesman of the Royal Government of Cambodia, said that these four solar power plants in Pursat province have a capacity of 30 MW, in Battambang province have a capacity of 60 MW, In Banteay Meanchey province, it provides 30 MW and in Bavet city, Svay Rieng province, it provides 20 MW.
Mr. Phay Siphan stated that the development of this additional solar power source is important and useful by providing additional power source to meet the shortage of electricity demand and enable the hydropower to store water in the pool. More for processing when needed.
The following is the report on the development of electricity in Cambodia for 2020 and 2021:
In the first half of 2019, increase imports from Vietnam by 50 MW and import from Thailand by 60 MW.
In August 2019, a 60-megawatt solar power plant will be commissioned in Kampong Speu province, and in November 2019, a 150-megawatt coal-fired power plant in Sihanoukville of CEL2 will be commissioned.
In December 2019, it will increase imports from Thailand by an additional 70 megawatts. - In November 2019, it will increase imports from Laos by 195 megawatts. Another megawatt plus 20 megawatts of solar power plant in Kampong Speu province.
In April 2020, a 400-megawatt black oil power plant near Phnom Penh will be commissioned and a 60-megawatt solar power plant will be commissioned in Kampong Chhnang province, which was approved by the Council of Ministers on May 5. Last April.
Therefore, according to our plan above, from the end of 2018 until the rainy season of 2020, we can add 1225 megawatts "reliable power source" plus solar power source, which we consider as "auxiliary power source". 140 megawatts, which is enough for 2020, which needs to be added only 770 megawatts.
From October to December 2020, a 60-megawatt solar power plant will be put into operation in Pursat province, which was approved by the Council of Ministers on April 5, plus a project to build a solar power plant. Four new solar projects with a total capacity of 140 megawatts are being requested and reviewed by the Cabinet.
