* How did the Syrian war break out?
Before the outbreak of the Syrian war, some Syrians complained and protested about unemployment, corruption and lack of political freedom under the leadership of Syrian President Bashar al-Assad. In March 2011, pro-democracy movements led protests in the southern Syrian city of Deraa, modeled on the Arab Revolution in several countries in the region.
At that time, the violent crackdown by the Syrian authorities on the protesters turned into a civil war, which was used by ISIS as a stepping stone to expand its influence in Syria until there was military intervention from outside countries such as The United States, Russia, Iran, Britain, France, Turkey, Saudi Arabia and Israel are all looking at their interests in Syria.
Who are the current rulers of Syria?
Last week, Syrian rights groups released a report accusing Russian warplanes and Syrian forces of intentionally targeting civilians by attacking hospitals in Idlib and Hama provinces, killing all. More than 500 civilians, including more than 100 children, have been fighting the two provinces since April. But both Russia and Syria have denied the allegations.
Idlib is home to more than 3 million Syrians. But since April, 300,000 people have fled their homes to escape the bombings that have fallen from the sky since April. The United Nations has repeatedly warned that the province could become a "bloody battle" between the Syrian army and Syrian rebels, which could lead to serious humanitarian catastrophe.
So, in conclusion, Syria is still in a state of war and insecurity, only the scale of the bloody conflict has been reduced due to the loosening of the hands of major countries involved in the conflict and the weakening of ISIS influence in the region. . In addition, since 2018, hundreds of thousands of Syrians have returned to their homes with the help of international organizations and the Damascus government.
