Discover: China's journey to Mars will begin in 2020
What Chinese scientists are worried about is Mars' atmosphere, which is only 1 percent of our Earth's atmosphere. During a mission to the moon, Chinese scientists once thought that if the moon had an atmosphere, it would have been easier to land a spacecraft there than the moon, as it is now, but return to Mars with the atmosphere. Things turned out completely contrary to what I had previously thought.
When Chinese scientists tried to develop a spacecraft to send to Mars, they discovered that the atmosphere on Mars is really a big problem. Although the vehicle does not require a lot of fuel to carry, this complex environment can cause uncertainties. Even so, the more complex the story, the more energetic scientists will be in developing technology.
Planned missions include orbiting landings and maneuvers on the surface of Mars. If the mission to Mars is successful, Chinese scientists expect to be able to explore the atmosphere and its many other features that will help provide clues to the origin and evolution of Mars as well as our solar system. As a whole.
Chamkarmon District Governor Brings Candles and Gifts to 4 Pagodas in Chamkarmon District
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Theng Sothol and his wife led officials at Chamkar Mon District Hall on July 9, 2019 to bring candles, gifts, food, equipment and some suffixes to the monks who remember the rain. Quarter to 4 pagodas in Khan Chamkarmon, Phnom Penh.
On this occasion, Mr. Theng Sothol mentioned the Buddhist tradition that in the Vesa festival, Buddhists often bring vase candles, food and other equipment to the monks at the pagodas to supply God. Monks, because this is the rainy season, a season full of rain, which monks have a hard time doing Niman Ben Bath, and on the other hand, this season is also a time when monks stay in the pagoda to study the Dharma.
The district governor and his family, together with the district officials, always bring candles, gifts and some equipment to the pagodas in the district to help alleviate the shortage of monks in the pagoda every year.
At the same time, the district governor also mentioned the continuous development of the country under the leadership of the Royal Government of Cambodia, with Samdech Techo Hun Sen as the Prime Minister, and this progress is inseparable from the field of Buddhism, which is always Buddhism and the empire must go hand in hand to build a progressive nation.
The Chief Inspector of Wat Than, on behalf of the monks, the pagoda committee and the Buddhists of the four pagodas, expressed his deep gratitude to the district governor and district officials for bringing donations to support the monks. This is an important contribution to facilitate the monks to stay and study during the rainy season during the quarter of the Buddhist season according to the Buddha's permission.
