(Phnom Penh): A delegation of Sri Lankan monks has visited Cambodia to learn about the progress and practice of Buddhism in Cambodia and to exchange views and experiences with each other in order to join forces to preserve Buddhism. Stable for the shadow of obedience of every Buddhist.
The Sri Lankan delegation of monks led by Prof. Dr. K. RahuLa Rahul, Director of the Institute of Pali and Buddhist Studies at Kalyani University of Sri Lanka, arrived in Cambodia on July 7, 2019.
In the afternoon of the same day, the delegation paid a courtesy call on Prince Prom Nath Nget at Wat Botvattey to better understand the growth of Buddhism in Cambodia, including the traditional culture of Cambodia.
Regarding the field of Buddhism, Samdech Preah Moha Sometheathipady Non Nget, a monk, ruled that the field of religion in Cambodia was completely lost during the Khmer Rouge regime. However, Chea Bon Kosal revived after the day of liberation on January 7, 1979, under the protection of the Cambodian government, especially the trust and active contribution of the Cambodian people throughout the country, the religious sector in Cambodia has grown as it is today. .
At present, in the ranks of monks, there are capable and knowledgeable monks in both Buddhism and general knowledge to continue to lead the field of Buddhism in Cambodia to grow and become even stronger.
Regarding the relationship between Cambodian and Sri Lankan monks in the field of Buddhism, Venerable Yorn Kosal, who is studying for a bachelor's degree in Sri Lanka, said that there are currently more than 100 Cambodian monks studying in Sri Lanka. Currently studying philosophy, Buddhism, Pali and Sanskrit from primary, secondary, high school and bachelor's degree.
