African swine fever spreads to Svay Rieng, Ministry of Agriculture and authorities launch measures to prevent the spread
(Svay Rieng): The African swine fever epidemic has now spread to Svay Rieng province, making it the third province to have such an outbreak after Ratanakkiri and Tbong Khmum provinces.
Mr. Men Vibol, Governor of Svay Rieng Province, said on July 8 that the African swine fever outbreak in Russey Chuor village, Krol Kor commune, Svay Chrum district, Svay Rieng province, had infected more than 100 pigs.
The provincial governor ordered professional officers and relevant departments to prevent the spread to other villages and communes.
The commune chief said that today, many pigs have died in a row, and he and professional officers burned two of them a few days ago.
Mr. Pen Chanthy, Director of Production and Veterinary Office of Svay Rieng Province, said that he and Mr. Sok Ros, Deputy Governor, were inspecting the outbreak site.
Separately, the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries has issued a letter to the Governor of Svay Rieng province about the outbreak of African swine fever.
In the letter, Minister Veng Sakhon requested the provincial governors and local authorities at all levels to cooperate in implementing the veterinary measures of professional officials to strengthen the prevention of movement of pigs, pork and pig-derived products. Outbreaks appear to be exacerbated during pregnancy and in children.
The African swine fever epidemic is not contagious to humans, but the disease has caused a high mortality rate of up to 100 percent, which could lead to high losses for pig farmers.
