"In order to join the Royal Government, the Bank has a specific vision for the market and has a clear marketing plan to develop and expand its potential based on a common spirit, to think together, to work together to guide all decisions to Achieve goals.
He added that MGN has high vision to become the leading digital bank in Cambodia, but will initially provide services such as: public deposit, credit, money transfer. And many other services.
(Tbong Khmum): Although the African swine fever virus does not affect human health, it poses a serious threat to the health of pigs. Contributing to preventing the spread of African swine fever to other farmers is essential and priceless. This is according to Mr. Cheam Chan Sophoan, Governor of Tbong Khmum Province.
Mr. Cheam Chansophoan said this on the occasion of inspecting and visiting the families of farmers affected by African swine fever in Soy and Kampoul Serey villages, Mean commune, Ou Raing Ov district, Tbong Khmum province and disseminating the consequences of African swine fever to young farmers in At noon on Saturday, July 6, 2019, which is to provide warmth as the head of the people's family.
Mr. Cheam Chansophoan also instructed the professional officers to continue to cooperate with the local authorities to continue to carry out biosafety, spray pesticides everywhere, prevent the traffic of pigs inside and out and through the outbreak area, and to monitor the health of animals in other places to avoid the outbreak. And really hard to prevent.
Mr. Ry Davin, Director of Veterinary Office of Tbong Khmum Provincial Department of Agriculture, said that in accordance with the Prakas of the Ministry of Agriculture and direct orders from the provincial governor, officials of the office continued to apply pesticides three times in 58 cages and On the streets in the village, especially to prevent the movement of animals in and out of the two villages, which are spreading disease within 3 km.
He said that to prevent this disease, pig farmers must spray disinfectants in the cage and the surrounding area regularly and keep the cage clean. Farmers need to keep sick animals separate from healthy ones. People entering and leaving the outbreak area should not enter the pigs' ponds of the disease-free farmers.
At the same time, it is necessary to prevent the transportation of live pigs from abroad or in the country without animal hygiene certificates from veterinary officials, whether by motorbike or car. If pigs are found to have a high fever, farmers should be extra careful as it could be a sign of African swine fever.
