Today marks the 11th anniversary of the inclusion of the Temple of Preah Vihear on the World Heritage List!
(Phnom Penh): 11 years ago, on July 7, all over Cambodia, including abroad, Cambodian men and women shouted! Candlelight vigil celebrating the inauguration of the Cambodian Temple of Preah Vihear on the UNESCO World Heritage List at the 32nd Session of the World Heritage Site in Quebec City, Canada, July 7, 2008 .
In particular, June 15, 2019 is also the 57th anniversary of Cambodia's victory over the Thai side in the Preah Vihear case under the ruling 57 years ago (June 15, 1962) of the International Court of Justice. The Thai side must withdraw its military and control of all its administrative territories from the disputed area at Cambodia's Preah Vihear temple.
According to the jurisdiction of the ruling, this judgment is in the non-protest period, meaning that the party with the right to rule is the legal owner. It was not until 40 years later that Thailand unilaterally established an area of 4.6 square kilometers, considering the land near the Temple of Preah Vihear to be under Thai sovereignty. The border dispute near the Preah Vihear temple area again occurred in July 2008 after some Thai troops briefly invaded Cambodian territory.
The International Court of Justice (ICJ) also upheld the verdict on the Preah Vihear temple on November 11, 2013, shortly after it heard oral interpretations from representatives of the Royal Government of Cambodia and Thailand in April 2013. By virtue of this ruling, the court ordered Thailand to withdraw its troops from the Preah Vihear temple area.
