Understand the law: ownership rights
(Phnom Penh): Private property in Cambodia was only gradually recognized during 1989 and later became more widespread when the 2001 Land Law was enacted. Village laws clearly define the nature of ownership in the Kingdom of Cambodia, as well as some of the rights of those who will become owners.
In addition, as the Civil Code is enacted, the provisions on property rights in Cambodia become clearer and the properties of certain properties become better for those who are entitled to ownership.
To better understand the legal awareness program of Fresh News in collaboration with the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation this week, please bring up the topic of "property rights" to explain to the people, government officials, students, what is ownership. And what rights does the owner have over his property?
Ownership refers to the full right to own property, not just land, but to all types of objects, both movable and immovable. Ownership may include the right to use, the right to enjoy and the right to dispose of these objects.
Legally, ownership arises from a legal possession or acquisition, such as through a legal purchase, exchange, or gift. Acquisition of any property that is not in accordance with the law is not possible.
The law generally defines the nature of a legitimate purchase, exchange, or gift in order to protect the producer, buyer, tradesman, or giver. For example, you bought land from someone who had a legal title to it.
Generally, when a person is named as the owner of a property, it also creates that ownership right to the owner. In the legal sense, a right is a legal power to act, use, enjoy, and dispose of one's possessions.
For example, land ownership is the legal power to use, enjoy, and dispose of the land in full ownership in accordance with the law, or in other words, the full legal right to own property. On the other hand, the right to own a immovable property is recognized in Article 44 of the Constitution of the Kingdom of Cambodia, which is the supreme law of the country.
Article 44 of the Constitution states that any person, whether individual or collective, has the right to own property. Only natural persons or legal entities of Khmer nationality have the right to own land. Legal private property is under the protection of the law. That will take away the property from any person, unless the public interest is required to do so in cases where the law provides and must provide appropriate and fair compensation in advance.
In general, forms of ownership are divided into several categories, as follows:
4. Co-ownership: is the ownership of immovable property of several persons divided into lots, each of which receives two shares, one is the respective private share, which must have full ownership rights and the other is a common share that must have the right to use Collectively.
5. Public property of the state: refers to public property that must preserve its natural image in order to preserve it for the benefit of society. This property cannot be traded and the ownership of this property cannot be determined by age. These properties include: forests, rivers, ports, railways, airports, roads, parks, parks, schools, hospitals, etc.
6. Private property of the state: private property of the state or property of public legal entities or property converted from public property of the state. State private property can be sold, divided or transferred as determined by law and must be done through a sub-decree, otherwise it will be considered a violation of the law. In addition, the state can take the private property of that state as a concession.
