(Kandal): A disabled soldier named Chao Sovanna and his family with four children released a video raising their hands and begging the Kandal provincial authorities to help solve their land dispute which was arbitrarily confiscated by the court in Koki commune, Kien Svay district Kandal province in 2009.
On July 6, 2019, Mr. Chao Veasna confirmed to Fresh News that he had asked Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, and Deputy Prime Minister Chea Sophara, Minister of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction, to help. His case has been resolved and Samdech Techo Hun Sen and the Minister of Lands have all been sealed for the lower authorities to look into this matter to resolve. However, the authorities did not address it.
He recalled that the execution of the verdict on August 14, 2009 in the demolition of his house to give to the other party is very unfair, which he has been protesting for 10 years, but has not. The results are still fruitful, because all the authorities seem to be in agreement with the other side.
The disabled soldier described that his parents' land had a valid title deed issued by the Kien Svay District Cadastral Office in 1995. His family's residence contains many documents such as Residence Book 112, dated January 11, 1999, family ID card, census list, letter from the National Authority for Land Management and the letter of intervention of the Ministry of Justice.
According to Mr. Chao Veasna, after the liberation on January 7, 1979, his parents came to live on that land. In 1981, a female named Preap Run came to ask his parents for a temporary stay, but the residence did not allow Preap Run to remain in the residence permit because the woman was elderly and under the support of Chao Veasna's parents.
Until 2005, another person named Svay Khon came to lead Preap Run to file a complaint to the Kandal Provincial Court for exploiting Chao Veasna's property, land and house. Remarkably, Mrs. Preap Run did not intend to act in violation of what Svay Khon led.
According to Mr. Chao Veasna, the reason for the dispute between him and Mrs. Preap Run was because the village-commune authorities at that time organized for the benefit of the land that belonged to his parents. He also concluded that the authorities at the time seemed to have benefited from Svay Khon.
Although Chao Veasna, who claims to have lived on his parents' land from the beginning until he had four children, has been trying to seek intervention for 10 years, his family has yet to receive justice. For this reason, he only held a banner urging the authorities to help find justice.
