According to excavations by experts, the Angkorian period had the longest pottery kiln in Southeast Asia.
(Siem Reap): According to a study by the APSARA Authority on the pottery of ancient people in the Angkor area, it is revealed that in the Angkorian period, there was the longest pottery kiln in Southeast Asia and the special features of Khmer pottery It is smooth and has a special texture than the pottery in Southeast Asia.
This statement was made in a lecture on "History of Angkor Thom research: a case study of pottery" on July 4, 2019 at the Angkor Conference Hall of the APSARA Authority with about 200 participants. : Tour guides, professors, youth researchers of the Heritage Education Project, officials and staff of APSARA Authority.
He said that in order to understand the above question, the researcher understands that it is necessary to study the pottery found from the excavation around Angkor Thom, because pottery is an important source to know the date and daily life of people. At that time.
On the other hand, pottery is an archetype that is not prone to destruction, can be on the ground for many years, especially pottery is constantly evolving, which can easily detect human activities.
Ear Darith, Deputy Director of the International Center for Research and Documentation of Angkor of the APSARA Authority and an expert on pottery, said, "There are two types of Khmer pottery: hard and brittle. The special feature of Khmer pottery is that it has a smooth layer. The Khmer pottery is even more special than the pottery in Southeast Asia, and the Khmer people used only wood or bamboo to paint.
Mr. Ear Darith added that the study of this story is very important to know the daily life of ancient people, as well as the type of people who use pottery. For the next generation should study the pottery used in ancient times to understand the Khmer civilization in more depth.
