(Geneva, Switzerland): Addressing the 41st session of the UN Human Rights Council in Geneva, Switzerland on July 4, 2019, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, emphasized that Nevertheless, Cambodia must continue to maintain peace and social stability.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen stated that "the experience of decades of devastating war has advised us that at all costs, Cambodia must continue to maintain peace and social stability."
Samdech Techo Hun Sen also said that in 2018, Cambodia held free, fair and just general elections with the participation of 20 political parties and the turnout was 83. 2%, a high figure compared to some countries that have long practiced democracy.
The election process was monitored by 500 international observers, 80,000 national observers and 9,000 party observers. Unfortunately, some countries did not send observers, but issued statements assessing the election as rigged and unfair.
On that occasion, the Prime Minister of Cambodia said, "I would like to ask the meeting to consider, if not visited and not seen in reality, how do those countries evaluate our election? "Both during the election day and after the election, the whole process went smoothly, without any violence or intimidation."
(Geneva, Switzerland): In front of the UN Human Rights Council at the 41st meeting in Geneva, Switzerland, the head of the Royal Government of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, affirmed that the Royal Government of Cambodia has never suppressed the right to freedom of expression. Of the people only encourages more because the Royal Government determines the opinions of the people as a mirror to evaluate the work results.
However, Samdech Techo Hun Sen emphasized that the Royal Government of Cambodia requested that all such statements be made responsibly and that there was a separation between the right to freedom of expression and insults or baseless accusations. Defamation, spreading false information, as well as calling for the overthrow of a legitimate government that could lead to social unrest and division.
"As the government, we just ask that all expressions be made responsibly and that there is a distinction between the right to express an opinion on an issue and the insult to accusation," he said. "There is no basis for prejudice, a window of reputation, the dissemination of false information, a call for the overthrow of a legitimate government that can lead to unrest and division in society."
Samdech Techo Hun Sen, who is known by the people as the strong man of Cambodia, also claimed that what is happening in Cambodia is that people from all political tendencies dare to express their opinions lively and without fear on all topics. The prince himself has always received a lot of feedback from the people.
The Prime Minister of Cambodia also highlighted that in relation to freedom of the press, Cambodia has 550 newspapers, 148 websites, 211 radio stations, 21 television stations, 113 cable TV channels and 39 journalists' associations. .
Due to all these factors, Cambodians have the right to receive information from all sources and to have the right to express their views on all topics. In fact, the Prince's Facebook page is a forum for people from all walks of life to participate in advising on local development issues, the misconduct of officials and authorities, and other issues. This is what Samdech Techo Hun Sen highlighted.
In addition, Samdech Techo Hun Sen said that Cambodia regularly organizes meetings with the media every year to strengthen cooperation between the Royal Government and the media in order to jointly fight corruption and contribute to the development of Cambodian society. Cambodian Prime Minister: Some countries did not actually participate, but evaluated the Cambodian election incorrectly (Video inside)
