The following is the full text of the Ministry of Labor's announcement: Agriculture Officer: A series of dead pigs in Ang Borei district, Takeo province, but no outbreak of African swine fever
The agriculture official's statement came in response to a recent case of dead pigs in Ang Borei district, Takeo province.
The head of the veterinary office of the Takeo provincial agriculture department confirmed that "the case of dead pigs in Takeo province was not caused by the African swine fever."
Mr. Ly said that so far more than 30 pigs have died in Prey Phkam and Ponley communes of Ang Borei district. After the dead pig case, the provincial veterinary officer sprayed the pig with blood and analyzed the pig's blood at the Phnom Penh laboratory, but the results showed that the dead pig was not caused by the African plague. No.
"Although we have not found any outbreaks of African plague, provincial veterinary officials have been monitoring the situation closely and have implemented safety and hygiene measures in all areas where pigs and pigs are kept," he said. "We have sprayed pigs and advised people who raise pigs to be careful about the spread of the disease."
According to the director of the Takeo Provincial Poultry Office, African swine fever is a deadly disease that has not been vaccinated against. He cooperated with the authorities at the border to prevent the import of pigs from neighboring countries.
On July 2, 2019, the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries confirmed that the African swine fever epidemic was re-emerging in two villages in Mean commune, Ou Raing Ov district, Tbong Khmum province after the outbreak. Already on Cambodian territory in Ratanakiri province in March 2019.
On July 2, 2019, the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries confirmed that the Ministry had identified two African swine fever outbreaks in Kampong Serey and Soy villages, Mean commune, Orang Ov district and Tbong Khmum province.
The Ministry of Agriculture has ordered the General Department of Animal Health and Animal Production to take immediate veterinary action at the outbreak site. New Preah Vihear Provincial Governor Leads Civil Servants and Citizens to Plant a Tree on July 9 in Choam Ksan District
