(Phnom Penh): Eighty-five Cambodian antiquities will be donated to the Royal Government and the people of Cambodia through Dr. Fumiko Takakova, a Japanese national who volunteered to return them to Cambodia.
The donation of 85 Cambodian antiquities will be organized by the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts on July 5, 2019.
According to a press release from the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts, the donation of the 85 artifacts by Dr. Fumikotakakova was dedicated to the spirit of her husband.
The Ministry of Culture stated that on behalf of the Royal Government and the people of Cambodia, I would like to express my deep appreciation and gratitude to Dr. Fumikotakakova for kindly handing over these artifacts to the Royal Government and the people of Cambodia and thanking him. Masamoto Fusino and his wife coordinated and directed the artifact.
Ms. Phoeung Sakona, Minister of Culture and Fine Arts, said that the return of the nation's jewels is the return of the souls of the Khmer ancestors, the great happiness of the nation. Voluntary return of artefacts to one's homeland demonstrates the appreciation of the cultural heritage of humankind and the improvement of the ethics of ancient collectors.
She added that on behalf of the Ministry of Culture, would like to encourage private individuals, museums, companies as well as institutions around the world who are exhibiting and preserving Cambodian artifacts to cooperate with the Royal Government of Cambodia through the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts, as well as through Cambodian embassies in each country voluntarily return the artifacts to Cambodia.
This statement was made by Samdech Techo Hun Sen on the occasion of the opening ceremony of the 7th Global Review Meeting on "Aid for Trade" at the WTO headquarters in Geneva, Thailand. Switzerland on July 3, 2019.
Samdech Techo further stated that Cambodia is actively playing a role in implementing the obligations and agreements of ASEAN to achieve the ASEAN Economic Community in 2015 and the vision of the ASEAN Economic Community to transform ASEAN into a common market and production base. Highly competitive, equitable development and integration with the world economy.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen's Request Made a Keynote Address at the Opening Ceremony of the 7th Global Review Meeting on "Aid for Trade" at the WTO Headquarters in Geneva, Switzerland This July 2019.
At the opening ceremony, Samdech Techo Hun Sen highlighted the important role of the World Trade Organization in helping the economic development of less developed countries, including Cambodia.
The Prime Minister stated that the WTO has established comprehensive rules to become the basis for global liberalization and for multilateral trade to ensure the stability of free and predictable trade flows. As well as mobilizing aid for national, regional and international economic development through six trade assistance review meetings that have already taken place.
The Prime Minister also said that the World Trade Organization has helped to promote economic diversification programs in developing and least developed countries and has helped increase international trade, create jobs and reduce poverty in Cambodia. In matters of highly effective member countries.
"I would like to commend and commend the WTO, as well as development partners and international organizations for their good cooperation in contributing to the least developed countries, including Cambodia, since the negotiations for membership in the WTO. World karma until now. This is the confirmation of Samdech Techo, the head of the Royal Government of Cambodia.
According to WTO principles, developed countries have an obligation to provide preferential treatment to least developed countries. According to this principle, Cambodia is one of the 36 least developed countries that receive preferential tariffs on its goods for export to developed countries such as European countries.
The Prime Minister urged the member states and development partners of the World Trade Organization to continue to provide and increase incentives through the multilateral or unilateral trade system. Continuing to provide and increase these benefits will also help less developed countries to get out of poverty faster in line with the 2030 Sustainable Development Goals.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen requested for careful consideration before issuing measures to suspend or remove this tax concession because it did not help the development of less developed countries.
(Phnom Penh): In preparation for the 2023 SEA Games and in response to the invitation of the Fujian Volleyball Management Center, a team of 21 Cambodian volleyball players traveled to Fujian, China for two months training. .
This cross-country training is supported by the Fujian Chamber of Commerce Friendship Association in Cambodia, as well as more than $ 100,000 from the Prince Real Estate Charitable Foundation.
Please note that Cambodia will host the 2023 SEA Games. In order to improve the strategy of the Cambodian volleyball team, the Fujian Provincial Volleyball Management Center has appointed Chinese volleyball coach Li Jun to train in Cambodia for 10 months.
To monitor the results of this training and under the auspices of the Fujian Chamber of Commerce Friendship Association in Cambodia, the Fujian Volleyball Management Center invited the Volleyball Federation of Cambodia to travel to Fujian, China to train for Two months in order to ensure that the Cambodian volleyball team can achieve better results in the upcoming 2023 SEA Games.
Since its establishment four years ago, the China Fujian Chamber of Commerce Friendship Association in Cambodia has been committed to contributing to the cultural exchange between Cambodia and China through academic support and other sponsorship activities.
Supporting the Cambodian volleyball team to train in Fujian this time will enhance the strategic level of the Cambodian national team and the development of the volleyball sector in Cambodia, as well as promote the transfer to the country. Outside of Chinese volleyball techniques.
The Prime Minister of Cambodia made the remarks in a keynote address at the opening ceremony of the 7th Global Review Meeting on "Aid for Trade" at the World Trade Organization in Geneva, Switzerland. On July 3, 2019.
Cambodia became a member of the World Trade Organization on October 13, 2004. Samdech Techo Hun Sen at the WTO headquarters highlighted the many benefits of being a member of the World Trade Organization in Cambodia's economic and trade development.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen stated that as a member of the World Trade Organization, Cambodia has strived to fulfill its role responsibly through the introduction and implementation of economic reform programs, laws, regulations and Rules to be fully compliant and consistent with international regulations, especially in line with Cambodia's commitments under the WTO framework.
In the field of trade, Cambodia has developed and implemented a Cambodia Trade Integration Strategy since 2001 and has continuously improved to meet the level of development of Cambodia. In 2019, Cambodia has completed the third and final update of the Cambodia Trade Integration Strategy 2019-223 to meet Cambodia's development needs as well as to the current and future context of regional and global trade.
Despite Cambodia's proud achievements, the Prime Minister affirmed that Cambodia has a vision to become a high-middle-income country by 2030 and a high-income country by 2050. To achieve this ambition, Samdech Techo Hun Sen called on all member states and development partners to consider providing further support in the following seven key areas:
Third, capacity building to take advantage of new growth sources, especially services and the adoption of the Industrial Revolution. Fourth, and to take advantage of the benefits of the digital economy.
Fourth, to develop a strategy to diversify the economy and diversify products to increase exports, which are a source of income for the promotion of women's well-being and employment.
Fifth, linking the policies, strategies and visions of the least developed countries with regional and global economic developments.
Sixth, to create, innovate and promote new practices by mobilizing additional funding from development partners and the private sector under the framework of this and other meetings.
Seventh, continue to provide assistance for reform and to support the implementation of trade facilitation agreements, especially on the reform of legal documents.
In his keynote address, the Prime Minister also highlighted some of Cambodia's efforts to implement and abide by WTO rules and principles.
Cambodia has also made efforts and authority to implement various obligations, including the obligation of notification and review of trade policy, twice in 2011 and 2017, as well as fulfilling its obligations. More than 70% of the provisions of the Trade Facilitation Agreement have been ratified and ratified.
Within the framework of ASEAN and the region, Cambodia has played an active role in implementing ASEAN obligations and agreements to achieve the ASEAN Economic Community - 2015 and the vision of the ASEAN Economic Community, which will transform ASEAN into a common market and production base.
