SEOUL (Reuters) - US Secretary of State Mike Pompeo has expressed hope that nuclear talks between the United States and North Korea could begin in mid-July. This was released by Yonhap News Agency on Monday, July 1, 2019.
Pompeo made the remarks in front of reporters at Osan Air Base in southern Seoul on Sunday, accompanying Donald Trump on a two-day visit to South Korea. "Nuclear talks could resume ... in July, I expect in the next two or three weeks," Pompeo said.
On Sunday, Donald Trump and Kim Jong Un met in a surprise meeting at the inter-Korean demilitarized zone. Through this meeting, Trump became the first US president to enter North Korean territory, where he and Kim agreed to resume nuclear talks.
