TOKYO (Reuters) - Japanese fishermen will resume whaling on Monday for the first time in more than three decades after a ban on Tokyo's decision to withdraw from the International Whaling Commission. . This is according to the release by CNA on Monday, July 1, 2019.
The resumption of whaling is expected to draw strong international criticism, especially from countries that oppose whaling, such as Australia. On the contrary, these criticisms have had no effect on Japan, as whaling and eating whales are considered by Japanese people to be their tradition.
A group of Japanese whaling boats will leave parts of Japan on Monday, including Kushiro on the northern island of Hokkaido. Critics have often accused Japan of using scientific research as an excuse to catch whales.
