(Phnom Penh): Mortgage is a legal regime of securing debt by the lien (pledged by the debtor). The importance of the mortgage is that the debtor does not have to transfer possession of the property that is the subject of the creation of the mortgage to the creditor, meaning that the debtor of the mortgage still has the right to continue living or doing business on his property.
In the spirit of Article 843, the creditors of a mortgage have the right to repay their debts with priority over other creditors to the immovable property of the debtor by a debtor or a third party without transfer of possession.
A mortgage is created by agreement between a creditor and a debtor or a third party (other than a party) who pledges its immovable property (as collateral). In the spirit of Article 844 of the 2007 Civil Code, there are no restrictions on the terms and conditions of the form of mortgage contract.
This means that the parties to the mortgage can make a verbal or written agreement. However, the Civil Code restricts the mortgage conditions of the mortgagee, the mortgagee may not take the mortgage to a third party other than the person who made the mortgage if he did not enter into the mortgage agreement in writing and not registered in the mortgage. The land register.
When a mortgage is created on a piece of land by a deed and registered in the land register, the mortgage applies to objects attached to the land, including buildings on the land adjacent to the mortgage. Whether the building was built before or after the mortgage was created (in the spirit of Article 846 of the Civil Code).
Pursuant to the provisions of Article 853 of the Civil Code, in the event that the mortgagee debtor fails to perform his obligations, the mortgage creditor may exercise his mortgage by forcibly applying for the sale of the mortgaged property to the court of first instance. Competence. However, the mortgage creditor can not exercise his right to apply for a forced sale to the court if the mortgage debtor performs his duties properly.
In summary, in order to create a mortgage, only if the creditors (lender) and the debtor (the borrower) agree and for the mortgage to be effective, applicable and able to compete with a third party, the mortgage must be made. In the form of a letter of authenticity and will be registered in the land register of the Department of Land Management, Urban Planning, Construction and Cadastral where the immovable property is located.
In addition, the establishment of a mortgage provides a lot of benefits to the debtor of the mortgage because the real estate that is the subject of the mortgage, the debtor does not have to transfer ownership to the creditor. On the other hand, mortgage debtors still have the right to enjoy the real estate that is the subject of their mortgage.
However, the debtor shall be liable to the creditor in the event that the debtor fails to perform and perform his obligations to the creditor, then the mortgage creditor has the right to apply to the court for a forced sale of the immovable property. Bring money to repay creditors. This is an important point that the law, especially the 2007 Civil Code, guarantees to protect the rights of mortgage creditors by repayment.
