After the entry into force of the EU-Vietnam trade agreement, tariffs of more than 99 percent, almost entirely on goods of both sides, will be withdrawn. Vietnam will levy about 65 percent of import duties on EU products, while the remaining tariffs will be lifted later in the coming decade.
According to the latest research report by the Vietnamese Ministry of Planning and Investment, the agreement (EVFTA) will help increase Vietnam's exports to the EU by 20% by 2020 to 42.7% by 2025. Nearly 44.4 percent by 2030.
In addition to providing excellent opportunities for the economy, the EVFTA will also ensure that trade, investment and sustainable development can be linked by setting the highest standards of performance, safety and environmental protection. As well as customers.
