Ambassador Wang Winthean: China will continue to provide cooperation and support in the field of mine action in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): On June 24, 2019, at the General Secretariat of the Mine Authority, a handover ceremony of the People's Republic of China for mine action equipment was held under the chairmanship of Senior Minister Ly Thuch, First Vice President of the Mine Authority, representing Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister. Of Cambodia and Mr. WANG Wentan, Ambassador Extraordinary and Plenipotentiary of the People's Republic of China to Cambodia.
On that occasion, Senior Minister Ly Thuch, on behalf of the Prime Minister and Chairman of the Mines Authority, thanked the Government and people of the People's Republic of China for their cooperation and support in the field of mine action in Cambodia by providing financial assistance and materials. And technical skills.
Every year since 2015, the People's Republic of China has provided assistance in the field of mine action through the Mine Authority, including mine clearance training, funding for the renovation of the ASEAN Mine Action Center (ARMAC), vehicles, motorcycles and computers. Minesweepers, armor, helmets, tents, problems (valves), wheelchairs and uniforms.
So far, 110 trainees from the Mine Action Authority and the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces (RCAF / NPMEC) have attended a training course on demining and explosive remnants of war from the Chinese People's Liberation Army Technical and Scientific University in Nanjing. .
At the same time, Mr. Wang Winthean said that China and Cambodia are good friends and brothers like hands and feet. The friendly and cooperative relations established and linked by the previous generation of leaders of the two countries have been constantly developed, and the strategic partnership has been continuously established.
"China will continue to strengthen ties and continue to provide cooperation and support in the field of mine action in Cambodia, including the sharing of technical experience, capacity building support, and the provision of search assistance materials," he said. "Effective ways and means to help Cambodia get rid of landmine threats, build new shelters, be safe and prosper."
It should be noted that Cambodia still has an area of 2,148 km2 under threat, including 919 km2 of landmines, 732 km2 of cluster munitions and areas of war explosives. The other 497 km2, with a budget of about 354 million US dollars.
The head of the Royal Government of Cambodia has agreed to set 2025 as the year when Cambodia is no longer threatened by landmines.
