(Phnom Penh): Mr. Kuch Kimlong, Deputy Prosecutor of the Phnom Penh Municipal Court, this morning, June 24, has been investigating the tycoon Sok Bun and his five accomplices in connection with the case of forgery of land on the island. Rong, Preah Sihanouk Province.
Tycoon Sok Bun, along with five other party members, was arrested by the Anti-Corruption Unit and sent to court on suspicion of systematically conspiring to sell community land from locals. According to a source from the court, Fresh News informed this morning, June 24, 2019.
An Anti-Corruption Unit investigator told Fresh News: "This is a case of serious damage to state property, intentional vandalism and corruption, abuse of power during 2016-2019 in Koh Rong commune. Koh Rong City, Preah Sihanouk Province. Five officials in Koh Rong and a businessman, Oknha Sok Bun, were arrested and sent to court this morning after being detained for 72 hours by the APEC.
Please note that Oknha Sok Bun has already been arrested once in connection with the case of violence against former TV presenter Ek Socheata alias Sasa in 2015. In this case, the court sentenced Oknha Sok Bun to three years in prison, but ordered him to serve only 10 months in prison. Secretary General of the Cambodian National Council for Children: Everyone has a duty to protect children from abuse, exploitation and violence against children
(Phnom Penh): Cambodia has many national and international laws, mechanisms and institutions to protect children from the use of violence, all forms of exploitation and sexual harassment. But it requires the participation of parents, guardians, teachers, monks, local officials and all citizens, not only the competent institutions and ministries, state institutions have the duty to protect children.
This is what Ms. Nhep Sopheap, Secretary General of the National Council of Cambodia for Children, said at the launch of the educational message on the protection of children from sexual abuse and harassment this morning, June 24, 2019.
Ms. Nhep Sopheap acknowledged that child abuse, such as the exploitation, violence and sexual abuse of children in Cambodian society, continued to be significant. The message also warns of the consequences of child abuse, and officials explain that everyone has a duty to protect children, not just the authorities.
In addition to physical and psychological violence, the rate of child sexual abuse is significant, according to a report. More than 6 percent of girls and 5 percent of boys between the ages of 13 and 17 reported having had at least one childhood sexual abuse.
After several rounds of debate with relevant partners, the message that has now been launched has seven tabs, including: From Together, Protecting Children from Sexual Abuse and Abuse Anyone who dares to exploit and sexually abuse children knows about prison and helps children from abuse, please report immediately to 1288 and 092 311 511.
